$DATUM 64-17-5
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Ketone (1.0mmol), nitrile (1.0 mmol), elemental sulfur (0.035 g, 1.1
mmol), Mg/ La mixed oxide (1mole %) and 2ml of dry ethanol were mixed and
placed in a 10ml pressure tube. The mixture was subjected to microwave
irradiation (MicroSynth, 160W, 150 psi, 78 °C, 85Hz) for the time
indicated in Table 1 and then diluted with chloroform (5 ml) and
filtered. The solid was rinsed with chloroform (2 × 3ml) and the combined
extracts were concentrated and purified by normal chromatography to
afford the corresponding 2-aminothiophenes. The spectral data (IR and
NMR) of all products matched in all respects with reported data. In the
case of conventional heating, the reactants, as above in 20ml ethanol,
were heated at reflux (78 °C) for the time indicated in Table 1. When the
reaction was complete (thin-layer chromatography; methanol/chloroform
1/9), solvent was removed by evaporation under reduced pressure and the
reaction mixture was diluted with 10ml of chloroform and filtered. After
evaporation of the solvent, themixture was purified by normal column
chromatography to give the desired products. Spectral data of synthesized
products are given in Table 3. Entry 1: Conventional yield: 91%. Ethyl
2-amino-4,5-dimethylthiophene-3-carboxylate. Mp: Found: 90°C; Reported:
91°C [3]; 1H NMR (CDCl3) δ 1.29 (t, 3H, J = 7.2 Hz), 2.20 (s, 3H), 2.36
(s, 3H), 4.30 (q, 2H, J = 7.2 Hz), 6.99 (s, 2H); 13C NMR (CDCl3) δ 9.8,
10.4, 14.1, 60.9, 112.9, 132.4, 134.9, 148.8, 165.7; IR (KBr) 1647, 3299,
3405 cm-1. Anal. calcd. for C9H13NO2S: C, 54.25; H, 6.58; N, 7.03; S,
16.09. Found: C, 54.28; H, 6.60; N, 7.07; S, 16.07.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM alternative conditions gave lower yield, Gewald reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Mg/La mixed oxide as an efficient heterogeneous basic catalyst for
synthesis of 2-aminothiophenes under microwave irradiation
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Moeinpour, Farid; Dorostkar, Nadieh; Vafaei, Majid
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synthetic Communications (2012), 42(16), 2367-2374

